{
  "rule": "divergence_duo",
  "metric": "Linf",
  "expected_min_order": 0.9,
  "notes": "Flux-form finite-volume divergence on a DUO icosahedral triangular mesh: (div F)_i = (1/area[i]) * sum_{k=1}^{3} edge_sign_on_face[i,k] * dc_edge[e] * F[e], e = edges_on_face[i,k]. MMS: constant cartesian field V=(1,0.37,-0.6); edge-normal flux F_e=V·n̂_e (circumcenter-chord normal); analytic surface divergence -2(V·r̂)/R. Like nn_diffusion_duo and divergence_mpas, asymptotic L-inf convergence is FIRST order, not second: the C-grid divergence is formally O(h^2) only on a uniform orthogonal mesh; on the irregular icosahedral-triangular mesh the circumcenter is not the area centroid and the edge midpoint is not the flux midpoint, so the per-cell truncation error is O(h) at the most distorted cells and dominates the L-inf norm (the L2 norm converges faster). Over the level 2 -> 3 -> 4 unit-sphere ladder the observed L-inf orders decrease toward the asymptotic 1.0; we require min order >= 0.9. See Ringler et al. (2010, J. Comput. Phys. 229:3065) and Thuburn et al. (2009) for the C-grid order analysis on unstructured meshes."
}
